## 3. Middle-dot separator is a literal character

$ python3 -c "... find 'setting %s/mo' in handlers_spending_optimizer.go ..."
b'setting %s/mo \xc2\xb7 starting living budget %s/mo (scheduled pha'
-> decodes to "setting %s/mo · starting living budget %s/mo (scheduled pha"

`\xc2\xb7` is the UTF-8 encoding of U+00B7 MIDDLE DOT — a literal text
character in the Go string, not an HTML entity, CSS ::before/content, or
image. It matches the separator convention already used elsewhere in this
same partial (e.g. "Below the minimum in 0.0% · 0 of 1,000 futures."), so
it is read the same way by a screen reader as the rest of the card's
evidence text (typically announced as "dot" or skipped as punctuation,
consistently across old and new sentences — no new pattern introduced).
Read aloud, the sentence works fine without the dot too: "Monthly Living
Expenses setting $X/mo starting living budget $Y/mo (...)" remains a
parseable, if slightly run-on, sentence — the separator is a visual/typo-
graphic aid, not load-bearing content.

## 4. Contrast (both themes, computed from the real built CSS)

Card background comes from guardrails.html's wrapper
(`bg-white dark:bg-gray-800`), confirmed via grep of
web/templates/components/whatif/guardrails.html:3 (unchanged from 7d0aec0).

$ python3 -c "... extract .text-gray-600 and .dark\:text-gray-300 rules from web/static/css/tailwind.css ..."
.text-gray-600 -> color:rgb(87 83 78)
.dark\:text-gray-300:is(.dark *) -> color:rgb(214 211 209)
.bg-white -> background-color:rgb(255 255 255)
.dark\:bg-gray-800:is(.dark *) -> background-color:rgb(41 37 36)

WCAG relative-luminance contrast computed in Python (sRGB -> linear ->
0.2126R+0.7152G+0.0722B, (L1+.05)/(L2+.05)):
- Light: text-gray-600 #57534E on bg-white #FFFFFF -> 7.63:1
- Dark: dark:text-gray-300 #D6D3D1 on dark:bg-gray-800 #292524 -> 10.18:1

Both clear AA body-text 4.5:1 by a wide margin in both themes. This is the
same class pair as the two pre-existing sibling secondary-text elements
(section 2), so this ratio was already carried by prior content on this
page — consistent with the brief's "contrast already audited" note.

